## 1. Introduction
Environmental challenges caused by organic wastewater pollution are an increasing threat to sustainable developments [1,2]. In recent years, advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) have been used for the removal of refractory organics. Sulfate-based advanced oxidation processes (SR-AOPs) are a more effective strategy than other AOPs [3–6].
MOFs have been extensively investigated in the area of organic matter removal from water by AOPs due to their high porosity, high specific surface area, abundant unsaturated sites, and structural stability [7,8]. Metal organic framework (MOF)-derived heterogeneous catalysts include single metal (e.g. Fe, Co, Cu) [9– 13], multiple metal (e.g. Fe/Co, Fe/Cu) [14–16] and MOF-derived catalysts (e.g. FeCu@C, Co/N). All of these catalysts exhibit excellent heterogeneous catalytic performance, but have limited practical applications. MOFs are generally synthesized in powder form, which can result in aggregation of small particles reducing the exposure of active sites. Immobilization of MOF nanoparticles (NPs) in a porous matrix with a high specific surface area prevents aggregation [12,17–20]. Thus, large specific surface areas were created by the MOF dispersion and stabilization, along with dispersion of active sites. In addition, combining MOFs with 3D substrates also resolved the problem of separation of MOF from solution [21,22]. The study of MOFs combined with porous polymer matrices has been extensively reported [23].Wang et al. [24] used in-situ growth to immobilize ZIF-67 on PAN and activate PMS to degrade organic pollutants. Additionally, studies reported MOFs loaded within zeolite beads for effective static and continuous flow degradation of tetracycline [25]. PSS was also selected as a support for the growth of ZIF-67 NPs, and the prepared composites showed excellent ability for the tetracycline removal [6]. These porous substrates contribute partially to the immobilization and separation of MOFs. However, the production of substrates is accompanied by complex chemical processes, high energy consumption and utilization of toxic chemicals, which inevitably have a negative impact on the ecosystem. Therefore, it is imperative to identify environmentally friendly and economical materials to fabricate MOF-based composites. Compared with fossil energy-based substrates, biomass offers significant advantages in terms of sustainability and environmental friendliness. The emergence of economical and environmentally friendly biomass has expanded the range of porous substrates available. A series of biomass/MOF composites with good adaptability and environmental friendliness was reported (Table S1). It was worth noting that MOF and biomass substrates were bidirectional and selective. Although a large number of MOFs have been developed, the MOFs that can be used to prepare $\mathsf{M O F}/$ biomass composites are limited to a few major MOFs families, taking into account the need to satisfy both the adaptability of biomass to water, temperature and chemical conditions, and the simplicity and low cost of synthesis [26]. The MOFs that have been reported so far are MOF-199 (named Cu-BTC), ZIF (Zeolitic imidazolate framework) family, the MIL (Materials Institute Lavoisier) and Zr-based MOFs [11,12,27–30]. Especially, Co-based MOFs, represented by ZIF-67 have been used as effective heterogeneous catalysts for the activation of PMS/PDS because of their abundance of divalent Co species, which exhibit excellent PMS/ PDS activation efficiency attributed to the particular 3d band structure of $C o^{2+}$
To date, ZIF-67 has been immobilized on numerus biomass substrates, such as wood aerogel [31], wood [32], cotton [33] and sugarcane [34] to activate PMS for the removal of organic pollutions. The starting biomass material for $\mathsf{M O F}/$ biomass composites can be either fibrillated cellulose or 3D cellulose scaffolds and is derived from sources such as trees and plants. The fibrillated cellulose includes fibers, microfibrils/nanofibrils, nanocrystals [28,35]. 3D cellulose scaffolds can be directly obtained from natural materials (such as wood, bamboo, and corncob) and scaffold by topdown synthesis [11,12,36]. MOF/cellulose hybrid membranes and nanocellulose/MOF aerogels with high porosity and excellent aqueous restoration properties have been synthesized using cellulose as the raw material in a bottom-up method [28,37]. In addition, a top-down approach to immobilize MOFs directly using hierarchical pore structure of wood has also been reported [12]. MOF composites obtained by removing lignin from sugarcane, wood, and corn straw as raw materials were used to prepare a lignin aerogel matrix for adsorption and catalysis [26,30,34,38,39]. Guo et al. [40] reported the in-situ growth of MOF in natural wood membranes for the removal of organic pollutants with greater than $96\%$ efficiency. Chen et al [41] impregnated wood with $\mathsf{P d C l}_{2}$ solution at $80^{\circ}C$ to obtain Pd NPs/wood, and the methylene blue (MB) degradation efficiency reached $99.8~\%$ at a treatment flow rate up to $\bar{1}\times10^{5}\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Cui et al [20] designed the POMOF/wood with up to $94.07\ \%$ removal efficiency for MB. Based on the abovementioned studies, wood can be used for water filtration due to its unique multilayered, interconnected pore structure and excellent hydrophilicity. However, in continuous-flow systems, wood filters frequently require additional power, such as a peristaltic pump or a vacuum pump to generate negative pressure [20,40]. The complex application conditions limit the widespread use of wood filters in actual water purification. Therefore, it is necessary to find an alternative substrate that not only has the advantages of wood but also facilitates liquid transfer without external forces.
With similar structure as wood, rattan has interconnected channels along the growth direction and a hierarchical pore structure. The vessel diameter of rattan varies between $27\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and $450~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , which is larger than the average fiber diameter of most wood species [42]. The unique microchannel structure contributes to the higher liquid flux of rattan compared to wood, greatly reducing the dependence on external forces for liquid passage. Therefore, ZIF-67@rattan was developed via a biocatalytic filter for in-situ growth of ZIF-67 using rattan as a biological substrate, to achieve gravity-driven high-throughput organic wastewater purification. The hierarchical pores and large specific surface area of rattan provide rich sites for dispersing and stabilizing MOF particles, which greatly prevents the aggregation of nanoparticles. The open and interconnected multi-scale pore structure allows to increase the water flux in rattan compared with that in wood, enabling directional transport without external forces. In addition, the rattan pore structure has a domain-limiting effect on the reaction. The activation of ZIF-67 on PMS occurred on the surface of channels with a large interface. The resulting reactive groups reacted rapidly with the contaminants flowing through the channels, promoting the effective interaction among the catalyst, PMS, and the contaminants, which in turn improved the degradation efficiency. Rattanbased filters exhibit excellent mechanical properties and can adapt to long-term water flow. It also exhibits good stability and reproducibility, and the recovery process is easy, avoiding catalytic wastes. Rattan is a green and sustainable resource, and the pretreatment operation is environmentally friendly, which can reduce the pressure on the ecosystem.
## 2. Experiments
## 2.1. Materials and chemicals
The rattan (Calamus manan Miquel) used in this study was obtained from Indonesia. Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $({\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}.$ $6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.99\ \%$ ), 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIm, $98\ \%$ , tetracycline (TC), phenol and peroxymonosulfate $(2\mathrm{KHSO}_{5}.\mathrm{KHSO}_{4}.\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}.$ , PMS) were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., ltd. 5,5- dimethyl-1-pirroline-N-oxide (DMPO) was purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., ltd. tert-butyl alcohol (TBA) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) were supplied by Nanjing Chemical Reagent Co., ltd. Methylene blue (MB) was obtained from Tianjin Chemical Reagent Research Institute Co., ltd. Deionized (DI) water was prepared using a laboratory ultrapure water machine. All chemical reagents were used without further purification.
Rattan pretreatment: The rattan cane was sliced into cylinders with a dimension of $(\Phi32\pm1.2)\times10\mathrm{mm}$ and rinsed a few times with DI water to remove surface contaminants. The samples were pretreated by the immersion in a $15\%\mathrm{w/v}$ NaOH aqueous solution for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The pretreated rattan samples were washed in DI water under constant agitation for about $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ until the pH value of the liquid reached 8–9.
## 2.2. Recirculating flow synthesis of ZIF-67@rattan
ZIF-67 nanoparticles were grown in situ on the walls of rattan microchannels by simulating the transpiration-dependent transport of water through rattan. Firstly, the rattan was placed into the tube of a homemade device, ensuring that the sides were sealed so that the liquid only flowed along the longitudinal direction. Next, the $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ solution (2.4 g, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ ; $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ , methanol) was pumped into the sample using a peristaltic pump at a flow rate of $50\ \mathrm{\mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , ensuring sufficient ionexchanges between $C0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions. After $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , 2-MeIm solution $\lbrace3.75\ \mathrm{g}$ , 2-MeIm; $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ , methanol) was added to the solution. The mixed solution was circulated in the rattan channels for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the samples were washed with methanol several times to remove excess precursors, and then placed in an oven at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The synthesis process was repeated several times, and the layer-by-layer assembly method was used to synthesize ZIF-67@rattan samples with different loadings. The obtained ZIF$67\textcircled{\em a}$ rattan samples were denoted as ZIF-67@rattan-X catalytic filters, where X was the layer-by-layer assembly cycles. (The sample ZIF- $67\textcircled{\omega}$ rattan-2 was used unless otherwise stated in this manuscript, i.e. ZIF-67).
## 2.3. Characterization
## 2.4. Catalytic activity tests
## 2.4.1. Batch catalytic experiments
The catalytic performance of ZIF-67@rattan was evaluated by the degradation of MB as a template reaction. MB was dissolved directly in DI water to prepare the water samples polluted with the organic dye $(20\mathrm{ppm})$ . During the batch catalytic experiments, all reactions were conducted under dark conditions. A specific amount of PMS was added to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of the MB solution and mixed thoroughly, followed by the addition of ZIF-67@rattan to induce the decomposition of MB and the catalytic reaction. The samples were taken at regular intervals and passed through a $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ syringe filter membrane. The filtrate obtained was quickly mixed with methanol to stop the reaction. The mixture was analyzed with a UV–vis spectrophotometer. In addition, the effects of temperature, PMS dosage and pH on the degradation of organic pollutants were also investigated. The pH of the mixture solution was adjusted using HCl or NaOH solutions $(0.1\mathrm{~M})$ . To study the reusability of the catalyst, the bulk catalyst was extracted from the solution using metal tweezers, washed several times with methanol and dried at room temperature.
## 2.4.2. Dynamic catalytic experiments
Dynamic catalysis was conducted with a homemade device under gravity. The cylindrical sample was placed in a homemade apparatus (Fig. S1), and the lateral side was sealed to ensure that the liquid was only transported axially along the rattan (with an effective flow-through area of $452.16\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ ). Filtration experiments were performed at atmospheric pressure without external pressure or energy. The fresh solutions containing MB $(20\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and PMS $\cdot1\ \mathrm{mM}\rangle$ were filtered across the ZIF- $67\textcircled{\em a}$ rattan filter. To analyze the effect of flux on the degradation of MB, a peristaltic pump was used to control the liquid flow rate and the sample was weighed on a balance to determine the actual flux of MB through the sample. The concentration of MB at the outlet after the reaction completion was measured with a UV–vis spectrometer. Reusability studies were similar to batch catalysis.
## 3. Results and discussion
The schematic of the 3D porous ZIF-67@rattan catalytic filter synthesis and its gravity-driven catalytic reduction of organic pollutants is illustrated in Scheme 1. Rattan is a fast growing plant, with hierarchical pore structure and high liquid flux. These excellent properties encouraged the preparation of high-speed water purification membranes. Inspired by the vertical transport of water from the roots during the natural growth of rattan, this work used the flow method to design rattan-based filters. The filters were prepared using a transport strategy to pump the MOF precursor solution through the rattan channels. The rattan components of cellulose and hemicellulose provided sites for the MOF nucleation. The MOF-NPs were deposited in situ along the interpenetrating microchannels of rattan to yield ZIF-67@rattan catalytic filters. The in-situ growth of MOF NPs resulted in uniform dispersion and high stability of the catalytic sites. This preparation method greatly reduced the use of the MOF precursor solution, decreased the environmental impact, and effectively reduced the cost of reactor preparation. As a result, rattan-based filters exhibited catalytic filtration driven by gravity, which is attributed to the long interpenetrating microchannels and the large specific surface area of the rattan. Notably, the block rattan-based microreactor directly resolved the problem of catalyst recovery.
## 3.1. Fabrication and characterization of ZIF-67@rattan
Native rattan exhibits a unique heterogeneous structure. The hierarchical pore structure of rattan can be observed after sawing along the growth direction. It mainly consists of vascular bundles embedded in the parenchyma cells of the matrix (Fig. 1a). The vascular bundle consists of xylem and phloem (Fig. 1a). The xylem comprised one large metaxylem vessel and a cluster of protoxylem vessels. The phloem comprises a number of sieve tubes with companion cells [18]. Microchannels arranged in vascular bundles can be observed in the cross section, with the inner surface decorated by pits of different sizes (Fig. 1b). Furthermore, the macropores of rattan account for approximately $33.29\ \%$ . The superior natural structure makes rattan an excellent choice for the preparation of biocatalytic filter. In this work, the flow preparation method was used to synthesize MOF NPs in situ in rattan microchannels. Alkali treatment of the rattan is essential prior to the preparation of the composite. Treatment with $15\ \%\ \mathsf{N a O H}$ ensured the ion exchange between the sodium cations and the intrinsic carboxyl groups in the rattan material, providing nucleation sites for the growth of MOFs. The zeta potential of the pretreated rattan samples changed from $-16\pm1.30\ \mathrm{mV}$ to $-31\pm1.32\mathrm{\mV},$ resulting in strong affinity for metal cations. As the metal precursors are continuously pumped into the pretreated rattan, $C0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ completed the exchange and anchor in the pore channels. Following the addition of organic ligand solution (2-MeIm), thin and uniform ZIF-67 NPs were generated on the inner wall of the rattan channels, which was supported by the color of the oblique section of the rattan sample (Fig. 1d, e) and EDS mapping results (Fig. 1d-5). The enlarged SEM image (Fig. S9) shows that the growth ZIF-67 nanocrystals can retain their dodecahedron morphology. This flow preparation increased the load of ZIF-67 (ZIF-67@rattan-2, 6.8 wt $\%$ ), being prevented the accumulation of ZIF-67 on the rattan surface causing pore blockage, and improved the internal functionalization of large rattan samples. It was also possible to increase the loading of ZIF-67 in the composite by the layer-bylayer columnar synthesis method (Fig. S7c). MOFs can also be synthesized in situ on the internal microchannels of rattan by impregnating the samples with MOF precursor solution under vacuum. However, for the same thicknesses of rattan, the loading capacity using conventional vacuum impregnation was $2.82~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , which was only $40\%$ of the capacity using flow synthesis. The flow synthesis greatly reduced the deposition of MOF NPs on the rattan surface. Therefore, the open channels at both ends of the rattan allowed the precursor solution to flow continuously, resulting in enhanced contact between the MOF precursors and the large surface of the channels, which provided enough reaction sites. In addition, the flow synthesis greatly reduced the use of precursor solution and is more economical and environmentally friendly. The results established the superiority of the flow synthesis method for the preparation of MOF/rattan composites.
The FTIR spectra were used to analyze the surface functional groups of rattan samples before and after the chemical treatment and the interfacial interaction between ZIF-67 and rattan. The results are presented in Fig. 2a, b. The characteristic peaks of the native rattan sample at 1245 and $1740\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the $c{-}0$ stretching of the aryl group in the lignin and the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching of the hemicellulose, respectively. The alkaline pretreatment obliterated the peak located at $1735~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The new absorption band at $1593~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{C O O^{-...}N a^{+}})$ indicated partial loss of hemicellulose and successful transformation from –COOH to -COONa after the alkaline treatment [12,43]. After the in-situ growth of ZIF-67 NPs, the absorption band at $422\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to the Co-N stretch mode of ZIF-67, indicating that ZIF67 was successfully assembled on the rattan [9]. The bands at 1382, 1417, and $1453~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated with the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ stretching mode of 2-methylimidazole, and the bands at 689 and $753~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the out-of-plane bending mode of 2-methylimidazole [44]. As shown in Fig. 2c, the XRD patterns revealed the crystal structure of the as-prepared samples. The ZIF- $67\textcircled{\alpha}$ rattan main diffraction peaks at 2h of 7.4◦, 10.4◦, 12.7◦, 18.1◦, and $26.7\circ$ corresponded to the (011), (002), (112), and (222), crystal planes of ZIF-67, respectively [24,33]. The two broad diffraction peaks centered around $2\uptheta=16.1^{\circ}$ and $22.3^{\circ}$ were associated with cellulose [45]. The above results indicated that ZIF-67 NPs were successfully formed inside the channels of the rattan and showed a high degree of crystallinity. As shown in Fig. 2d, the thermal stability of native rattan and ZIF-67@rattan was investigated via TGA in an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The results showed that the decomposition temperature of ZIF- $67\textcircled{\em a}$ rattan was slightly lower than that of the native rattan, with $21.8\mathrm{~\%~}$ residue, which was $7.6\%$ higher than that of the natural rattan, confirming the presence of ZIF-67 NPs in the rattan channel arrays.
The surface electronic and chemical properties of ZIF- $67@$ rattan samples were analyzed via XPS. The XPS spectra of the native rattan showed the presence of C and O with the main major photoelectron peaks at 285.1 eV (C1 s) and 532.8 eV (O 1 s), respectively (Fig. 2e) [46,47]. The peaks at 399.4.8 eV (N1s) and 781.4 eV (Co2p) were observed in the ZIF-67@rattan samples, further demonstrating successful combination of ZIF-67 with rattan [2]. The XPS spectra of Co $2{\tt p}3/2$ peak derived from $C0^{2+}$ species are the characteristics of ZIF-67, as shown in Fig. 2f. The several peaks located at $785.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $803.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to the shake-up satellite (marked as ‘‘Sat.”) [48]. The high-resolution C 1 s spectra can be deconvoluted into three peaks, i.e., ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ (284.5 eV), CAOAC (285.74 eV), and $0-C=0$ (288.7 eV) (Fig. S11a) [32]. The high-resolution O 1 s spectrum (Fig. S11b) shows three peaks at binding energies of approximately 531.8, 532.6, and $533.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which can be attributed to Co-O, $c{-}0$ , and $0-C=0$ , respectively [34]. Fig. S11c shows the three peaks of N 1 s centered at 398.9 and $400.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ attributing to –NH and Co-N species. This result further proved that 2-MeIm and $C0^{2+}$ successfully coordinated to form ZIF-67 nanoparticles in the ZIF- $67\textcircled{\omega}$ rattan catalytic filter. The highresolution spectrums of C 1 s and O 1 s of the rattan are shown in Fig. S12a and b.
## 3.2. Mechanical properties
Mechanical properties of catalysts are crucial for practical application and industrial scale-up. Sufficient strength and flexibility of both continuous flow catalysis and catalyst recovery are needed to preserve the multiscale pore structure during the constant water flow impact. Therefore, the mechanical properties were examined via the compression and elastic fatigue tests. Compression tests in the longitudinal direction of the samples showed that the pretreated rattan exhibited a lower strength $(13.12\ \mathrm{MPa})$ than the native rattan (25.13 MPa). However, a slight increase in the modulus of elasticity was observed under the compression along with a 0.5-fold strain-failure value (Fig. 3a). Rattan tests showed a slight increase in flexibility after the pretreatment owing to the partial removal of the cell wall matrix components, i.e., lignin and hemicellulose. The compressive strength of ZIF-67@rattan was 17.36 MPa. The loading of ZIF-67 crystals increased the mechanical properties of the materials. Meanwhile, rattan is a viscoelastic material, both elastic and plastic. Under low levels of stress, the rattan showed elastic properties. In this experiment, the elastic limit stress of ZIF- $67\textcircled{\em a}$ rattan was 257.91 N, and the stress induced by water flow was within the elastic range. The 1/2 load at elastic yield was selected for repeated compression studies according to the ultimate elastic stress of different samples. The results showed that the three materials exhibited superior flexibility and porosity even after the repeated pressure treatment and constant use (Fig. 3b). Notably, the rattan material provided a robust and resilient falsework for the ZIF-67 NPs, providing excellent mechanical properties for the rattan-based filters. The compressive strength of the filter membranes reported in this study is higher than that of the previously reported chemically synthesized polymer-based MOF materials[49–53].
## 3.3. Catalytic performance
## 3.3.1. Catalytic performance of different catalysts
The catalytic performances of different catalysts, including rattan, ZIF-67@rattan, ZIF-67@rattan/PMS and only PMS, were investigated via the MB degradation. The results are shown in Fig. 4a. PMS alone failed to show effective degradation, and only $5.20\ \%$ of MB was degraded within $25\ \mathrm{min}$ , which indicated poor selfdecomposition [2,33]. The rattan showed a significant MB removal rate, with an adsorption efficiency of $33.1\ \%$ at $25{\mathrm{~min}}$ . Furthermore, the addition of PMS had little effect on the removal efficiency of MB, indicating that the natural rattan cannot activate PMS. Adsorption played a critical role in the catalytic degradation of MOF-based materials [54]. The removal efficiency of only ZIF$67@$ rattan was $36.86\%$ , which was a slightly higher removal rate than that of the native rattan, suggesting that the composite still retained the adsorption capacity of rattan after the alkali treatment and the loading of ZIF-67 NPs. The ZIF-67@rattan/PMS catalytic system showed excellent degradation of MB, and rapidly degraded $97.4\%$ of MB within $0.5\mathrm{min}$ initially, followed by complete degradation at $3.5\ \mathrm{min}$ . The reaction rate constant $(k)$ was $1.74~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , indicating that ZIF-67@rattan had highly activity for the PMS activation which comparable with ZIF-67 powder. The results showed that the in-situ growth method can largely preserve the activity of MOFs and overcome the drawbacks of MOFs powders in practical applications. Total organic carbon (TOC) is a common-used method to evaluate the water purification process for the organic pollution. Hence, TOC removal rates of these catalysts are presented in Fig. 6c. The result showed the ZIF-67@rattan/PMS system still had the highest removal efficiency of organic carbon with a TOC removal rate of $82.63\ \%$ . When only ZIF- $.67\textcircled{\em a}$ rattan was presented in the system, the removal of TOC by adsorption alone was $34.65\ \%$ , counting a reduction of $49.98\ \%$ The TOC removal rate for any catalyst loaded natural rattan was $17.66\ \%$ The above results showed that the composites were able to remove MB to some extent by adsorption, but the addition of PMS greatly increased the organic degradation mineralisation rate. The ZIF67@rattan/PMS system achieves ultra-fast MB degradation within 1 min and has an efficiency that matches the high liquid flux of the rattan material, a combination that allows the composite to achieve efficient water purification at high fluxes.
Then the performances of ZIF-67@rattan-1, ZIF-67@rattan-2, and ZIF-67@rattan-3 were compared in batch experiments. It was shown that the loading of ZIF-67 in the composite gradually increased as the number of layer assemblies increased (Fig. S7c). The increase in loading resulted in more total catalyst in the system, leading to an increase in degradation efficiency (Fig. S6a and b). The amount of cobalt ions leached during use is an important factor in the evaluation of the catalyst. The high loadings were accompanied by higher leaching of cobalt ions, and the leaching of ZIF-67@rattan-3 was $1.368\mathrm{mg/L}$ , being higher than the specified $1\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ (‘‘Emission standard of pollutants for copper, nickel, cobalt industry”, GB 25467–2010). Therefore, combining catalytic efficiency and cobalt ion leaching, the ZIF-67@rattan-2 sample was used in this study.
Rate constant $k$ was obtained via the pseudo-first-order kinetic equation at different temperatures, as follows [33]:
$$
\ln{\frac{C_{0}}{C}}=-\mathbf{k}\mathbf{t}
$$
where ${\sf C}_{0}$ is the initial MB concentration and C denotes the realtime MB concentration.
3.3.2. Effects of initial $p H_{\cdot}$ , temperature, and PMS dosage on MB degradation
In order to further analyze the catalytic performance, the effects of the initial pH value, PMS dosage, and temperature on the degradation efficiency were investigated. Although SR-AOPs exhibit a wide pH range, it is still important to optimize the initial pH of the reaction. In this experiment, the pH value ranged from 2 to 10. Fig. 4d shows that the ZIF- $67\textcircled{\omega}$ rattan/PMS system exhibited a more prominent degradation effect on MB under a wide pH range. When the pH value exceeded the 4–10 range, almost all of MB was degraded within $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ , which can be used to promote the industrial application of composite materials. Meanwhile, in this range, the increase of $\mathsf{p H}$ value facilitated the degradation of MB. When the pH value was 10, the complete degradation of MB lasted only $2.25\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the $k$ was $3.386\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , indicating that the alkaline environment facilitated the degradation of MB. The high pH corresponds to high concentration of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ , which favors electron donation and thus spontaneous decomposition of PMS, resulting in additional ROS for the MB degradation [1,9,55]. However, when the solution was too acidic $\mathrm{(pH}=2\mathrm{)}$ , the degradation efficiency tended to decrease significantly, but still robust, with a degradation rate of $80\%$ within $9\mathrm{{min}}$ . The poor performance under acidic pH was attributed to the formation of $\mathsf{C o O H}^{+}$ (Eq. (1)), which limited $\mathsf{S0}_{4}\bullet^{-}$ generation and excessive $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ may result in the stabilization of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ on ${\mathrm{HSO}}_{5}^{-}$ (Eq. 2), which adversely affected the PMS activation [56,57]. The $\mathsf{p H}$ range of the actual wastewater ranges between 4 and 9. The ZIF-67@rattan/PMS system showed a good degradation effect in this pH range, indicating that the system had strong environmental adaptability for possible industrial applications.
$$
\mathrm{CO^{2+}\mathrm{+H_{2}O}\rightarrow C o O H^{+}\mathrm{+H^{+}}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{HSO_{5}^{-}}+\mathrm{SO_{4}}\mathrm{\cdot^{-}}\rightarrow\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}+\mathrm{SO_{4}}\cdot^{-}+\mathrm{H}^{+}
$$
The degradation temperature has a significant effect on the reaction kinetics. In this study, the experimental temperature was close to the actual effluent temperature. The reaction rate constant increased with the increasing temperature, indicating that the PMS activation was a heat-absorbing process [33]. When the temperature was $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , MB was completely degraded within $2\ \mathrm{min}$ and $k$ reached its maximum value $(1.651\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ) (Fig. 4c).
Since the release of sulfate ions can cause secondary environmental pollution, it is vital to analyze the PMS dosage. The PMS dosage of $0.5{-}1.5\operatorname{mM}$ was selected to analyze the degradation efficiency of the system. Fig. 4b reveals the impact of the PMS dosage on the MB removal. A PMS dosage of $0.5~\mathrm{mM}$ decreased the degradation efficiency, and the degradation rate only reached $84\%$ in $6\ \mathrm{min}$ . The degradation efficiency gradually increased with the increasing amount of PMS, and the degradation rate reached the maximum at $1\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathit{'}k=1.53\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ). Subsequently, the degradation rate showed a decreasing trend when the PMS dosage was further increased. An appropriate increase in PMS concentration can generate additional free radicals to degrade organic matter, whereas excessive levels of PMS saturated the catalyst surface, thus reducing the catalytic activity. Hence, the $1\mathrm{mM}$ PMS concentration was deemed appropriate for catalytic degradation.
## 3.3.3. Flow filtering catalytical property
In this study, the directional flow of liquid in the filter was only dependent on its own gravity, contrast to the most wood-based catalytic filters and synthetic membranes which need certain amounts of external pressure [20,40,41,58,59]. The gravity-driven was relied on the excellent natural structure of rattan. Rattan had a larger vessel diameter and a more coherent water transport system in the growth direction than wood (Fig. S3). In order to validate the catalytic degradation performance of the rattan-based filter under gravity, the total degradation in the flow state and the degradation efficiency at different flow rates were investigated. The microscopic pore structure of the ZIF-67@rattan composite can be utilized in the gravity-driven water treatment with the enhanced catalytic degradation of organic pollutants. Compared with the total levels of dynamic and static degradation, the total degradation via flow catalysis was about 5.2 L $20\ \mathrm{ppm})$ for the same ZIF-67@rattan, which was $1.7\mathrm{L}$ higher than that in the static catalysis. Therefore, the flow catalysis demonstrated the synergistic effect of the combination of highly porous rattan with ZIF-67 NPs. As shown in Fig. 5a, b, the neatly arranged microchannels in the rattan facilitated the rapid passage of solutions and provided an abundant specific surface area and active sites for most catalytic degradation reactions. In addition, rattan exhibits other excellent natural structures, such as spiral thickening that can be observed in protoxylem vessels (Fig. 5a), perforated plates and pits (Fig. 5b) in metaxylem vessels, and pits in parenchyma cells. The liquid flow (Fig. 5c, d) through these structures promoted the interaction between the catalyst and the contaminant, facilitating the catalytic degradation. In addition, this study investigated the degradation of organic pollutants by the production of SO4∙- and ∙OH, which have a very short half-life in water and a greater concentration of radicals on the catalyst surface than that in the bulk aqueous phase [60,61]. The rapid directional flow of the solution greatly increased the contact between the pollutants and the catalyst loaded in the rattan channels, thereby avoiding the waste of radicals and increasing the total amount of degradation.
The reaction time affects the efficiency of dynamic catalytic degradation of pollutants as the solution passes through the aligned rattan microchannels. By adjusting the flow rate of the contaminant solution through a peristaltic pump, the relationship between the MB $20\ \mathrm{ppm})$ flux and degradation rate was investigated. As shown in Fig. 6a, the degradation rate increased firstly and then decreased with increasing flux in the range of $5463.6-$ $24492\ \mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The fastest degradation rate of $55.76~\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ was achieved when the flux was $10173.6\ \mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The flow rate determined the residence time of the solution in ZIF-67@rattan, so it was meaningful to select the most reasonable flow rate, which promoted the interaction between pollutants and catalysts to the maximum extent and improved the reaction efficiency. Notably, the superior natural structure of rattan provides an efficient water filtration performance for the final biofilter. Indeed, both liquid flux and filtration efficiency of the ZIF- $.67\textcircled{\em a}$ rattan composite reported in this study were substantially higher than those obtained through the widely-synthesized polymeric membranes and biofilters in the previous reports (Table S2). In addition, the catalytic degradation of the liquid was driven by gravity through the ZIF-67@rattan, which greatly simplified the filtration process, reduced energy consumption and made the purification process environmentally friendly compared to the filters that require additional equipment such as vacuum pumps or monojet syringe to provide the drive energy (Table S2).
The rattan-based filter in the dynamic catalytic system can be effectively reused and easily separated from the reaction system.
The pipe with rattan-based filter was removed from the unit and placed in a dry environment, and the rattan was separated from the pipe due to dry shrinkage, and then cleaned with methanol for further use. As shown in Fig. 6b, the activity of the filter decreased with the repetition times while still maintained $82.43\%$ of the original catalytic activity after ten repetitions, showing excellent reproducibility. Possible causes of reduced catalytic performance were small erosion of ZIF-67 due to methanol cleaning and alteration or destruction of the active catalytic site during repeated use.
## 3.3.4. Potential degradation mechanism
In our rattan-inspired filter, the rattan alone showed no significant catalytic degradation of pollutants but promoted the catalytic behavior of ZIF-67 by anchoring the ZIF-67 NPs on microchannel walls. This prevented ZIF-67 NPs from accumulating and leaching. Unlike wood or other biomass materials, rattan has a unique hierarchically porous structure with xylem vessel diameters ranging from $27~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to $450~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ [42]. The neatly aligned long, multi-scale and accessible channels of the rattan microreactor showed excellent permeability and provided a natural advantage for the auto gravity-driven circulation of fluid. Perpendicular to the flow direction, perforated plates and pits additionally increased the interaction and diffusion between the reactants, allowing complete diffusion of the reactants into the narrow channels of the rattan to react with the catalyst (Fig. 5c, d).
Most previous studies attempting to improve catalyst productivity focused on the design and synthesis of catalysts, but due to the limitations of mass transfer and radical lifetime, the reactions occurred on the surface of the catalyst rather than in the aqueous phase [61]. Designing catalytic degradation systems under spatial constraints to minimize mass transfer constraints for short-lived species can partially improve catalytic efficiency [65]. Significantly, the natural microchannels of rattan limit the reaction to a narrow channel, which increases the interaction between the reactants and improves the reaction efficiency. Further, the rapid flow of the pollutant solution along the microchannels of the rattan results in rapid reaction away from the catalytic surface, which attenuates the effects of mass transfer limitation on the degradation efficiency partially.
## 3.3.5. Stability and practical applications
Cycling stability is an important index of catalytic performance, affecting the market prospect of catalysts. For powder catalysts, the recovery usually requires complex centrifugation or filtration, which can lead to powder aggregation and reduced activity, as well as catalyst leakage, resulting in secondary environmental contamination. Compared with the powdered catalyst, the block form of ZIF-67@rattan, which is based on the rattan template, completely avoids the complexity of the separation process. ZIF-67@rattan can be directly removed by tweezers and soaked in methanol for
$30~\mathrm{{min}}$ after use, then rinsed with DI water repeatedly and dried at room temperature for reuse. In order to evaluate the reusability and stability of ZIF-67@rattan, six consecutive MB degradation experiments were conducted under the same reaction conditions. After 6 cycles, the removal rate of MB was $98\ \%$ indicating its excellent stability (Fig. 4f). To further investigate the stability of the composite, the TOC removal rate was tested under continuous flow conditions. The results showed that after ten cycles (with a total treatment volume of approximately $17\ \mathrm{L}$ ), the TOC removal rate of the ZIF-67 system was $69.34\mathrm{~\%}$ , which was a reduction of $13.29\%$ compared to the first use (Fig. 6c). Though it had a slight decrease compared with the first degradation process, ZIF$67\textcircled{\omega}$ rattan catalyst showed decent cycling stability ability and maintained a stable mineralisation capacity for pollutants. The decreased degradation efficiency may be due to the shielding on the active sites of the catalyst from the adsorption of intermediate product and a bit of active material leaching [9]. Moreover, the XRD and FTIR patterns (Figs. S4, 5) show that there was no significant difference between the used catalyst and the fresh catalyst. The crystalline phase and chemical structure of ZIF-67@rattan still remained constant after the four-cycle degradation process. Meanwhile, SEM-EDS images of catalyst after usage were also provided as shown in Fig. S6. It was indicated that the morphology and structure of the ZIF-67@rattan catalyst after the usage almost had no obvious destruction because its original typical characteristics were maintained. The results illustrated the reason of the decent cycling stability of ZIF-67@rattan.
Meanwhile, the simplicity of separation and recovery of ZIF$67@$ rattan suggests its potential for industrial applications. The leaching of metal ions limits the application of catalysts to a certain extent, in hence it is important to investigate the leaching of cobalt ions in continuous catalytic systems. The results showed that the leaching of cobalt ions tended to decrease during the operation and the leached cobalt ions were detected at $0.163~\mathrm{{\mg/L}}$ after the tenth cycle, which was far below the specified value $(1~\mathrm{mg/L})$ according to the standard of ‘‘Emission standard of pollutants for copper, nickel, cobalt industry (GB 25467–2010).
The actual wastewater system is very complex and contains a wide range of organic pollutants and microorganisms. In order to verify the general applicability of ZIF-67@rattan, the degradation of other representative pollutants was also studied, such as rhodamine B (RhB), tetracycline (TC), and phenol. As shown in Fig. S2, the degradation of RhB, phenol and TC can reach $100\%$ in
$60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , indicating that the ZIF-67@rattan system can be used to decompose various organic pollutants. In order to establish the potential for practical applications of ZIF-67@rattan, the purified MB solution configured with three real water samples was selected for analysis. There were many natural organic matters (humus acid) and ions $[\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf P}{\sf O}_{4}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ) in the actual water bodies. The results showed that ZIF- $67\textcircled{\omega}$ rattan exhibited excellent catalytic performance in real water samples and had great potential for practical applications.
The excellent natural structure of rattan enables ZIF-67@rattan to achieve gravity-driven degradation, greatly simplifying the devices used. Moreover, the composite still showed excellent catalytic degradation in natural water. These features made the composite to play roles as both filtration and reactor simultaneously. In the practical use, the effluent can be purified by simply placing it at the location of the outlet, as shown in the conceptual diagram in Fig. 8.. The load of ZIF-67 limits the application of filters in quoted water purification though. Meanwhile, as a biological material with no negative health effects, rattan can help provide safe drinking water in resource-limited environments if its water purification capacity is increased in a healthy way along incorporating easy filtration operations.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a natural 3D porous rattan decorated with ZIF-67 NPs was demonstrated to function as an excellent gravity-driven catalytic filter owing to the synergistic effect of well-distributed ZIF-67 nanoparticles and channel microstructures of rattan. The open and hierarchical pore structure of rattan provided an excellent platform for immobilisation and dispersion of ZIF-67 nanoparticles and activated PMS for catalytic degradation. Inspired by the transpiration of rattan, the rattan-based catalytic filter was prepared at room temperature via the simple continuous-flow method, resulting in a uniform distribution of ZIF-67 nanoparticles on the rattan channel inner surface. ZIF- $67@$ rattan retained the pore characteristics and mechanical strength of the natural rattan, facilitating contact and interaction between the pollutants/ZIF-67/ PMS in the channels, which improved the catalytic activity for the degradation of organic pollutants. In the flow-through catalytic test, the gravity-driven filter performed strong catalytic degradation at a high flux of $10173.6\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and showed good stability, maintaining $82.43\ \%$ catalytic activity after ten cycles. In addition, the filter was used to treat environmental water samples with high catalytic degradation capacity. In summary, this novel ZIF$67@.$ rattan catalytic filter exhibited excellent catalytic activity and stability in the degradation of common organic pollutants, and operated under gravity obviating the requirement for energy inputs. Based on the structural advantage of the rattan, this gravity-driven catalytic strategy is expected to be utilized to fabricate other catalysts for continuous water repair as well as application in other continuous catalytic reactions.
